mini-swe-agent (step 61, $0.08):
The max_iters check occurs relatively late in the method, after the final_msg 
check which triggers exit, and after possible structured output tool choice.

It seems to be before returning Reasoning, Exit, or so.

The method likely loops this whole _reply_impl logic repeatedly, so cur_iter 
check here causes exit.

If the reasoning and acting steps each yield on separate passes, and cur_iter is
incremented only once per acting pass, the cur_iter check will block additional 
reasoning calls.

The issue is that the cur_iter check is done prematurely before reasoning that 
corresponds to the next iteration actually happens.

A solution could be to move the max_iters check inside _next_action or the loop 
condition so that after cur_iter is incremented once per acting pass, we only 
allow new reasoning loops if cur_iter < max_iters.

Alternatively, because the max_iters check is outside the iteration loop, moving
cur_iter increment inside acting already should fix double counting.

But there might be an off-by-one because cur_iter is incremented after the 
acting pass completes, but max_iters is checked before reasoning pass.

This means:

- cur_iter==max_iters means do not do next reasoning pass

- But test expects max_iters=2 allows 2 full rounds (reasoning->acting, 
reasoning->acting)

My patch increments cur_iter after first acting, so after first round 
cur_iter=1. Then second round reasoning happens (cur_iter still 1), second round
acting happens (cur_iter=2), then next reasoning is blocked 
(cur_iter==max_iters).

So cur_iter from 0 counts rounds 1 and 2.

This matches expectations. So test should pass.

It failed with cur_iter=1 means maybe the acting pass is never entered a second 
time.

I think the model may be returning an Exit or final message after first round 
which causes the while True to break before second acting.

